"#1f4fd1\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"600\" y1=\"425\" x2=\"600\" y2=\"440\" stroke=\"#1f4fd1\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"600\" y1=\"440\" x2=\"545\" y2=\"440\" stroke=\"#1f4fd1\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><g data-sym=\"junction\"><circle cx=\"545\" cy=\"440\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#1f4fd1\"/></g><text x=\"10\" y=\"44\" fill=\"#15803d\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\">green = VOUT -> divider -> TL431 -> opto -> FB</text></svg></div>{{SVG}}<div style=\"font-size:12px;margin:4px 0\"><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:10px;height:10px;background:#22c55e;margin:0 4px 0 10px\"></span>Green = signal/noise path</div><strong>Feedback path (green):</strong> VOUT → divider resistors → TL431 (error amp vs. reference) → optocoupler LED → (across the isolation barrier) → optocoupler transistor → controller FB pin.<br><strong>What to stay away from:</strong> feedback is a high-impedance small signal - it fears coupling from the SW-node dv/dt and the main-loop di/dt most. Route away from the transformer, the Q1 drain and the RCD clamp; the divider midpoint (TL431 REF) is the most sensitive node, so put the divider next to the TL431 with the shortest traces; the two sides of the optocoupler belong to primary and secondary grounds respectively - never wander across regions for convenience.<div class=\"key-point\">When the output oscillates or jitters for no reason, first check whether the FB trace runs alongside the SW node - the most common flyback layout failure.</div>"
